<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pinnata Castra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pinnata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castrum">Castra</a>, 'the winged camp', is a place in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> listed in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 CE among the towns of the Vacomagi. Its location is uncertain: it has been identified with the promontory fort at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghead">Burghead</a> on the Moray coast and with the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress at Inchtuthil on the Tay.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ptolemy gave the name in Greek as Pteroton Stratopedon, meaning winged camp, which has been rendered back into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Pinnata Castra or sometimes Alata Castra; the Ravenna Cosmography lists it in the locative as Pinnatis. Ptolemy counts it a polis, or town. Whether it was a native stronghold or a Roman site is uncertain: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> liked to place their sites close to established native ones and to give them names related to the earlier settlements.</p><p>No purely native settlement in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> is named by Ptolemy, though such places are listed in unoccupied Ireland, and most Roman sites in the north were temporary marching camps, which were not normally named. Pinnata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castrum">Castra</a> was one of four points in Britain that gave Ptolemy astronomical data, its longest day recorded as 18.5 hours against 17 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, the furthest north of his readings. As the northernmost polis on his list it may mark the limit of the advance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a>'s army, the likely ultimate source of his data.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1922 the archaeologist Ian Richmond identified it, on place-name and archaeological grounds, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress at Inchtuthil north of Perth, which he excavated and believed to have been built with merlons, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> pinnae. O. G. S. Crawford supported this in 1949, and it is still the identification given by the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><p>Later scholars rejected it, since Ptolemy's coordinates place Pinnata Castra on the Moray coast, and both he and the Ravenna Cosmography put it near Tuesis, generally identified with the River Spey. Plotting his known sites suggests a place just east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghead">Burghead</a>, and a forerunner of the later Pictish fort at Burghead remains a strong candidate. Other suggestions include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a>, possible Roman camps at Thomshill near Elgin or Balnageith near Forres, Cluny Hill in Forres and a lost site in the Culbin Sands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pinnata Castra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He lists four settlements in their land, Bannatia, Tamia, <b>Pinnata Castra</b> and Tuesis, more than for most northern peoples, suggesting a well-populated territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/vacomagi">Vacomagi</a></li></ul></section>
